<section class="ph"><div class="ph-media"><img class="ph-img" src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="797" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap ph-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Contents &amp; Personal Property</span></nav><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Appraisal service</p><h1>Contents Claim Appraisal in Michigan for Personal Property</h1><p class="ph-lede">After a fire or water loss, your belongings are often valued with generic prices and steep depreciation. We review the contents valuation line by line and support a fair amount.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-maize" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-glass" href="/request-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>Typical perils</dt><dd>Fire, smoke, water, sewer</dd></div><div><dt>Main disputes</dt><dd>Pricing, depreciation, cleanability</dd></div><div><dt>Your key task</dt><dd>Room-by-room inventory</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Why contents claims are so hard to get right</h2>
<p>After a fire, flood or major water loss, the house is only part of the claim. Furniture, clothing, electronics, tools, kitchenware, books, decorations and keepsakes all have value, and most people have never inventoried them. Insurers often apply heavy depreciation, assign generic replacement prices or treat items as cleanable when they aren’t. That’s where contents disputes begin.</p>
<h2>Building a defensible inventory</h2>
<p>A strong contents claim starts with a room-by-room list. For each item, the useful details are:</p>
<ul>
<li>description, brand and model where known;</li>
<li>quantity and approximate age;</li>
<li>condition before the loss;</li>
<li>a current replacement price for a comparable item;</li>
<li>photos, receipts, bank or card statements, and online order history.</li>
</ul>
<p>Old photos and videos of holidays and birthdays are surprisingly helpful for showing what was in a room. So are manuals, warranty cards and boxes stored in the basement or garage. Work through one room at a time, starting with the rooms you remember best, and add items as family members recall them.</p>
<h2>How depreciation and settlement work</h2>
<p>Many Michigan policies pay contents at replacement cost, but only after you actually replace the item. Until then, the insurer pays actual cash value, which subtracts depreciation for age and condition. Disagreements arise over useful-life tables, how old an item really was, and whether a like-kind-and-quality replacement price is realistic. The <a href="/mi-claim-calculators/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV and ACV holdback calculator</a> shows how much may be recoverable once you replace.</p>
<h3>Special limits</h3>
<p>Policies commonly set lower limits for categories such as jewelry, firearms, silverware, cash, collectibles and business property kept at home. Scheduled personal property endorsements cover these differently. Appraisal values items within the policy’s terms; it doesn’t change those limits.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Don’t discard damaged items too soon</b>Your insurer may want to inspect damaged contents. Photograph everything before disposal, and ask in writing whether the insurer needs to see items before they’re thrown away, especially after sewage or smoke exposure.</div>
<h2>Cleanable or a total loss?</h2>
<p>After smoke or contaminated water, the question of whether an item can be cleaned is central. Soft goods exposed to sewage, mattresses and upholstered furniture soaked in floodwater, and electronics exposed to corrosive soot may not be restorable. Restoration costs can also exceed replacement cost for lower-value items, which makes replacement the more reasonable measure.</p>
<h2>How we handle contents appraisal</h2>
<p>As your appraiser, we review the insurer’s contents valuation line by line, check pricing against current market comparables, challenge depreciation that doesn’t match an item’s condition or useful life, and identify items left off. Contents often accompany a building dispute after a <a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/fire-smoke-loss-appraisal/">fire</a> or <a href="/michigan-claim-appraisal/water-sewer-backup-appraisal/">sewer backup</a>, and we can handle both parts together so the appraisal panel sees the whole loss.</p>
<p>Unfamiliar terms like like kind and quality or actual cash value are defined in our <a href="/insurance-claim-terms/">claim terms glossary</a>. We are independent appraisers, not public adjusters or attorneys, and we don’t give legal advice.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary><span>What if I don’t have receipts for my belongings?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Receipts help but aren’t the only evidence. Photos, videos, card statements, online order history and manuals can all support an inventory.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Why is my contents payment so much lower than replacement cost?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Insurers usually pay actual cash value first, subtracting depreciation. If your policy pays replacement cost, the held-back amount is typically released after you replace items.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Can appraisal increase my jewelry limit?</span><i class="qa-plus" aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>No. Appraisal sets the value of the loss, but special category limits in your policy still apply unless items were scheduled separately.</p></div></details></div>
